  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 236 PM EST Fri Nov 6 2026\n\nVisible satellite imagery highlights scattered stratus and cumulus \nover the region. These are tied to a broad trough over the Great \nLakes region within the northwest cyclonic flow. Moisture is limited \nand precipitation is not expected outside of a few sprinkles. High \ntemperatures will top out in the mid to upper 60s this afternoon. \nForecast soundings hint at a non-zero chance for patchy dense fog \ndevelopment in SW VA and NE TN, but scattered skies likely linger \novernight tonight that could help limit fog development. \n\nOn Saturday, there is a signal for weak frontal passage that will \nbring another round of scattered clouds Saturday afternoon and \nevening. A few sprinkles can't be ruled out but dry low level air \nwill likely limit actual precipitation. Temperatures top out in the \nlow 60s. Sunday will be similar, but slightly warmer and mostly \nclear skies. The week ahead starts out with mild & dry weather \nconditions as we will have to look upstream for signals of a pattern \nchange. The pattern begins to change on Wednesday as a deep trough \ndigs in across the West Coast mid-week. This will amplify the mid-\nlevel pattern to with an impressive ridge forming over the west-\ncentral NOAM. We will see an trough drop out of Great Lakes region \nand cut off over the SE CONUS as an omega-like blocking pattern \ntakes shape. Where this trough is centered over will likely see \ncooler, cloudier pattern with an increase chance of showers. For us, \nour highs will cool from the upper 60s in the first half of the week \nto the upper 50s and lower 60s in the second half. We'll monitor the \nsituation to see how the finer details play out, but it's safe to \nexpect cooler, cloudier weather as we head into next weekend.",
